{
  "abstract": "We reassess whether the weak mixing angle provides a redundant test of the selected MTT gauge profile. Let r_{21}=I_2/I_1=g_1^2/g_2^2, with g_1=\\sqrt{5/3}\\,g_Y. Then \\sin^2\\theta_W=3r_{21}/(5+3r_{21}) at the same scale and in the same scheme. For the selected SMDR profile at Q=M_t, this gives \\sin^2\\theta_W=0.2346644\\pm0.0000433. Because r_{21} was itself obtained from the measured (g_Y,g_2) profile, this equality is an exact algebraic round trip, not a held-out prediction. Supplying an absolute g_2 from (G_F,m_W) does not remove that circularity. A genuine redundancy test requires an MTT source theorem selecting r_{21} without electroweak gauge data, followed by independent common-scheme transport. The obsolete one-loop 5~\\mathrm{TeV} calculation and its precision-prediction claim are withdrawn.",
